{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>I did not know who did this.</b> Even now that you have informed me that the theft was committed by my servants, I cannot determine which of them did this, for none of them is suspect in my eyes — for if one were suspect, he would not dwell in my household." "<b>And you did not tell me, nor did I hear.</b> You reproached me that while I was king over you, it was incumbent upon me to investigate the theft. Yet a king does this only for one of two reasons: either because of the outcry of the victim, or because of public rumor publicizing some theft that has occurred. Now, you did not tell me, nor did I hear any public outcry about this."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "Reading חמוץ in the Hebrew as cognate with חמס/המון (outcry/public uproar); the translation follows the contextual logic of the two reasons Avimelekh then specifies — the victim's cry and public rumor."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:31:05.268823486Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}